<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1970s–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EPLF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eritrean People's Liberation Front was the armed movement that won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Formed in the early 1970s by groups that broke away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>, it built a disciplined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> army based in the mountains around Nakfa. It took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in May 1991, and after the 1993 referendum its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a> became president; in 1994 it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-front-for-democracy-and-justice">People's Front for Democracy and Justice</a>, the sole legal party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Eritrean People's Liberation Front" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Permjak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_EPLF.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
